# tools/ales_stereo_echo_witness.rish — stereo_echo: the feedback delay carried into stereo, the same delay and feedback on both channels, each channel reading and feeding back through its own live buffer — the first rung of the stereo time-based wing (ALES181).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES181.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-085411_fill-ales181-lotus-stereo-echo.md
# Stands on: lotus/echo.rye (ALES66 — the mono feedback delay run once per channel, its EchoError reused whole and its precheck (newly factored out) reused for the up-front atomic validation), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip),
# lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and the one true saturate the mono echo reuses).
#
# lotus/stereo_echo.rye echoes a stereo master: it runs ALES66's proven mono echo on BOTH channels with the SAME delay and feedback — each sample the dry input plus a fraction fb_num/fb_den of the output one delay earlier, y[i] =
# saturate(x[i] + fb·y[i-delay]). The stereo modulation class is whole (ALES176–180: the amplitude pair — tremolo, ring modulator — beside the delay-line trio — vibrato, chorus, flanger); this rung opens the stereo time-based wing with
# its primitive exactly as ALES66 opened the mono one — one primitive, then compositions. It is the natural crux next: the flanger (ALES180) already reads its own live output through a feedback path, and the echo is the plain longer
# feedback delay that whole wing stands on, the simpler live-feedback carry (a fixed whole delay, no LFO). The same delay and feedback on both channels, each on its own live buffer, is the linked stereo echo, both trains decaying in
# lockstep. The delay is a sample count and the feedback a named fraction the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the image for free; mono echo reads the delayed output off
# the clip buffer itself, so run once per channel the left feeds back through the left's buffer and the right the right's, never crossed — no cross-channel feedback, and because the delay is at least one whole sample buf[i-delay] sits
# strictly before the write index (an already-written output, the dry audio, or silence), so an echo needs no carried state. Feedback below unity decays and a constructive sum past the rail saturates once per channel; because each
# channel's feedback loop stays wholly within its own buffer, an identical-channel master stays identical. Honestly noted: like the flanger and unlike the snapshot rungs, the echo does not preserve an exact panned integer ratio, the
# feedback's @divTrunc truncation drifting it by ones — expected of an accumulating feedback delay, so the image law proven is the identical-channel one, the sense a mix cares about most. It validates the delay, feedback, and span once
# against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadDelay, BadFeedback, BadRange), so a refusal never echoes one channel and leaves the other torn; EchoError reused whole. Values only, never a length; the two channels leave
# balanced. The crux is three laws: the stereo echo law (each channel equal to ALES66's mono echo with the same delay and feedback byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing), the dry-identity law (a zero-feedback echo the dry
# master on both), and the image / balance / silence / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_echo_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-echo: Language EN — stereo_echo echoes a stereo master by running ALES66's proven mono echo on both channels with the same delay and feedback: each sample the dry input plus a fraction fb_num over fb_den of the output one delay earlier, y at i equals saturate of x at i plus fb times y at i minus delay. The stereo modulation class is whole at ALES176 through 180; this rung opens the stereo time-based wing with its primitive exactly as ALES66 opened the mono one, one primitive then compositions. It is the natural crux next: the flanger already reads its own live output through a feedback path, and the echo is the plain longer feedback delay that whole wing stands on, the simpler live-feedback carry, a fixed whole delay with no LFO. The same delay and feedback on both channels, each on its own live buffer, is the linked stereo echo, both trains decaying in lockstep. Mono echo reads the delayed output off the clip buffer itself, so run once per channel the left feeds back through the left's buffer and the right the right's, never crossed, no cross-channel feedback, and because the delay is at least one whole sample the delayed read sits strictly before the write index, an already-written output, the dry audio, or silence, so an echo needs no carried state. Feedback below unity decays and a constructive sum past the rail saturates once per channel; because each channel's feedback loop stays wholly within its own buffer, an identical-channel centred master stays identical. Honestly noted, like the flanger and unlike the snapshot rungs, the echo does not preserve an exact panned integer ratio, the feedback's truncation drifting it by ones, expected of an accumulating feedback delay. It validates the delay, feedback, and span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never echoes one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-echo: Style Radiant — the echo is the foundation of the whole time-based craft, the primitive a keeper's chorus, flanger, and reverb are each a delay line read a different way of. ALES66 opened the mono wing with the feedback delay, each sample returning heard again a while later a little quieter, and the flanger of ALES180 already reached into its own live output through a feedback path — so the echo, the plain longer feedback delay the flanger's zero-depth case reduces to, is the honest rung to seat the stereo wing's base, the simpler live-feedback carry with a fixed whole delay and no moving LFO. This rung runs ALES66's proven echo on each channel with the same delay and feedback, so both trains decay in lockstep and the image is held; each channel reads and feeds back through its own live buffer, the left through the left's and the right through the right's, never crossed, so there is no cross-channel feedback and no carried state, because the delay is at least one whole sample so the delayed read sits strictly before the write index. Feedback strictly below unity decays, each echo smaller than the last, and a constructive sum past the rail saturates once per channel. Because each channel's feedback loop stays wholly within its own buffer, an identical-channel centred master stays identical, the image law the sense a mix cares about most; and honestly, like the flanger and unlike the snapshot rungs, the echo does not preserve an exact panned integer ratio, since the feedback path accumulates truncation and a right exactly half the left drifts from that ratio by ones once the loop has fed a few samples, which is expected of an accumulating feedback delay rather than a linear scaler. The crux is three laws: the stereo echo law, each channel equal to ALES66's mono echo with the same delay and feedback byte for byte with the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed; the dry-identity law, a zero-feedback echo equal to the dry master on both channels so the feedback genuinely governs the train; and the image, balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law, an identical-channel master staying identical, an all-silent master echoing to silence on both with the lengths held, a bad delay, a bad feedback, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, and count zero the identity on both."
say "ales-stereo-echo: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 clips (left, right), each rung through ALES66's own live-buffer feedback delay, one delayed read and at most one multiply-divide per sample run in i64 and each channel reading and feeding back through its own buffer, the fed sum saturating once; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (an echo writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the delay, feedback, and span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (ALES66's precheck, factored out of the mono echo and shared, mirroring the ALES73 flanger refactor), so a refusal never echoes one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoEchoError is ALES66's EchoError (BadDelay, BadFeedback, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault. The stereo echo law is proven by running both stereo_echo and ALES66's mono echo on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the dry-identity law proves a zero-feedback echo equals the dry master on both; and the image, balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an identical-channel centred master staying identical (with the honest note that the accumulating feedback delay does not preserve an exact panned integer ratio, the truncation drifting it by ones), an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the three refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the delay a count of sample indices rather than milliseconds and the feedback a named fraction below unity rather than a decibel, no cross-channel feedback, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_echo"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_echo.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-echo: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_echo.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-echo: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-echo: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-echo' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-echo: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-echo line"
